      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Multiple sclerosis (MS) is an autoimmune disease characterized by inflammation and axonal degeneration of the central nervous system and a leading cause of disability in young adults. The matrix metalloproteinases in general and specially gelatinase B/metalloproteinase-9 (MMP-9) plays a role in the pathogenesis of multiple sclerosis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate the presence of the MMP-9 -1562C/T polymorphism in a Portuguese population of MS patients and assess its impact in susceptibility and course of the disease. The relation of MMP-9 serum levels with the polymorphism and with clinical and therapeutic factors will also be assessed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Our study included 355 Caucasian individuals distributed as MS patients (n=169) and controls (n=186). Samples were genotyped for -1562C/T polymorphism by PCR-RFLP analysis. MMP-9 concentration in serum was analyzed using a commercially available enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "A significant increase in T-allele frequency was found in female MS patients, but not in the total patient population. No association between the presence of the polymorphism and disease progression was found. MMP-9 serum concentrations were increased in patients, and although not influenced by the -1562C/T polymorphism, were modified by INF-beta therapy."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Although we did not find an association of this polymorphism with disease susceptibility or prognosis, MMP-9 appears to be a good therapeutic response marker for multiple sclerosis."
          }
        ]
      },
